Text-based reasoning inside agent loops can consume thousands of tokens before an agent ever decides to invoke an external tool. Hybrid Reasoning Agents introduce a viable alternative by allowing models to execute intermediate thinking entirely in continuous latent representations, switching back to tokens only when interacting with external environments.

The system uses an architectural bridge called HybridLink along with a continuous working memory. Across 11 benchmarks, this dual-mode execution cut inference latency by 1.8x to 2.6x and dropped token usage by over 35 percent, all while boosting benchmark accuracy by 10.4 percent over purely token-based reasoning.

Instead of wasting context window bandwidth on verbose scratchpads, models can compress complex planning into latent activations and decode only the executable tool calls.

Latent reasoning bridges the gap between fast internal cognition and verifiable external action.

