title: Row locks with joins can produce surprising results
source: hn
url: https://hakibenita.com/postgres-row-lock-with-join
date: '2026-10-18'
authors:
- Haki Benita
why_read: Understand how concurrent row updates and locks in PostgreSQL joins can unexpectedly cause valid rows to disappear from query results. You will learn the mechanics behind this edge case and concrete strategies to prevent it.
hn_id: '50143989'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50143989
---

PostgreSQL row-level locking during joins can quietly return zero rows even when your foreign keys guarantee that the joined record exists. When executing a SELECT FOR UPDATE query that joins a child record to its parent, PostgreSQL locks rows in the exact sequence determined by the query execution plan.

If a concurrent transaction updates or moves the parent row between the time PostgreSQL evaluates the join condition and the time it acquires the row lock, the query re-evaluates the join predicate against the updated row version. If that row version no longer satisfies the filter or join condition, the query silently drops the row from the result set instead of blocking or raising an error.

This behavior frequently causes race conditions in financial ledgers, inventory deductions, and status state machines. Standard defensive patterns like enforcing NOT NULL foreign keys cannot prevent this anomaly under default read committed isolation.

To eliminate this concurrency hole, lock the parent row explicitly before evaluating dependent joins, or upgrade the transaction isolation level to repeatable read.

Understanding your query plan lock acquisition order is essential for true data integrity.

