title: How causal attention preserves cached token representations across turns
source: hn
url: https://www.sithankanna.com/posts/prompt-caching-vs-kv-caching.html
date: '2026-10-18'
authors:
- hdit
why_read: Read this to understand the mechanistic reasons why prompt caching works across multi-turn transformer inputs. You will learn how causal attention masks ensure previously computed key-value pairs remain invariant when new tokens are appended.
hn_id: '50143965'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50143965
---

Prompt caching is often treated as simple key-value memoization, but its correctness hinges on a specific architectural property of transformer layers. When you append new tokens to an existing prompt, earlier key and value vectors do not change.

In a multi-layer transformer, layer outputs mix representations across tokens. It is easy to assume that appending token D alters the internal states of preceding tokens A, B, and C in deeper layers. However, because causal attention masks zero out all future positions, the representation of earlier tokens at layer N depends strictly on tokens at or before that position at layer N minus one.

Because token C cannot attend to future token D, its intermediate hidden vectors remain identical across turns. Consequently, the linear projections that generate keys and values remain unchanged. Inference engines can safely skip the prefill stage for identical prefixes without invalidating downstream representations.

Understanding causal invariance at the layer level makes optimizing multi-turn agent latency straightforward.

