---

Moving the entire network and dispatch loop of a GPU server into the Linux kernel does not improve latency or reduce CPU utilization compared to an optimized epoll server in userspace.

Engineers combined sockmap BPF programs, sleepable io_uring operations, and persistent CUDA graph execution to eliminate all userspace thread wakeups. In theory, bypassing syscall context switches and memory copies should produce substantial tail latency savings for short GPU tasks like micro-LLM generation or cuBLAS routines.

Careful benchmarks proved otherwise. The kernel-driven loop performed almost identically to a lean userspace baseline. The bottleneck for lightweight GPU tasks does not stem from userspace context switching. Instead, it is dominated by driver synchronization, PCIe bus interaction, and kernel network stack packet handling.

Removing userspace threads sounds like an easy win, but eliminating the wrong overhead yields zero performance gain.
